---
import Callout from "../../components/Callout.astro";
import PageShell from "../../components/PageShell.astro";
import { TimeAllocationShift } from "../../components/TimeAllocationShift";
import MainLayout from "../../layouts/MainLayout.astro";
---

<MainLayout title="Time Allocation Changes - AI Training Guide">
	<PageShell chapterId="development" subChapterId="time-allocation-changes">
		<div class="prose space-y-6">
			<p>
				AI-assisted development is usually faster overall, but that does not mean
				your job becomes smaller. It means the shape of the job changes. You spend
				less time typing every line yourself, and more time writing requirements, documentation, code review, and testing
				real behavior.
			</p>

			<p>
				A good mental model is that you are working more like a <strong
					class="text-stone-800">senior developer reviewing code from a fast junior
					developer</strong
				> than an individual contributor writing everything from scratch. The
				output comes quickly. However, you are still the owner of this code. You need to be able to answer to it and you are responsible for the quality of the code.
			</p>

			<h3>How the Work Shifts</h3>

			<p>
				In traditional development, most of the effort is concentrated in the act of
				writing code. With AI, some of that time moves earlier into requirements and
				context, and some of it moves later into review, testing, and cleanup. The
				total cycle is often shorter, but the share of time spent on oversight goes
				up significantly.
			</p>

			<TimeAllocationShift client:only="react" />

			<p>
				This is why strong AI-assisted development often feels different from what
				people expect. The leverage does not come from avoiding thought. It comes
				from spending less time on first-draft implementation and more time on the
				parts that require judgment.
			</p>

			<ul class="space-y-3 my-4">
				<li>
					<span class="font-semibold">Requirements and prompting matter more.</span>{" "}
					If the AI starts from a vague task, you get fast output in the wrong
					direction. A clearer brief saves more time than a faster model.
				</li>
				<li>
					<span class="font-semibold">Code review becomes core work, not cleanup.</span>{" "}
					You are looking for incorrect assumptions, silent failures, weak naming,
					missed edge cases, and places where the model overbuilt the solution.
				</li>
				<li>
					<span class="font-semibold">Testing carries more weight.</span> AI is very
					good at producing plausible code. That makes manual verification and
					targeted testing more important, not less.
				</li>
			</ul>

			<Callout variant="tip" title="Especially true for UI work">
				<p>
					On UI-heavy tasks, the shift is often even more pronounced. Models can
					produce working interface code quickly, but visual polish, spacing,
					responsiveness, interaction details, and design taste still need careful
					human review.
				</p>
			</Callout>

			<p>
				If you embrace that shift, AI becomes much more
				useful. The goal is not to watch it write code. The goal is to direct work,
				review output rigorously, and use the speed gain to ship better software
				faster.
			</p>
		</div>
	</PageShell>
</MainLayout>
